# interplay_lifewalk_2: Seeded walks that land device lifecycle events in live mixed work (pgtest/lifewalk_util.py): each seed is one scenario, interplay_lifewalk_<seed>, whose program the generator builds from the seed alone.  Each round of a walk builds a live state and lands one lifecycle event in it. The live state composes what the earlier walkers drive apart: GPU execs on the root FIFO and worker child FIFOs, chained into order witnesses (dagwalk, randmix), some held behind a gate FIFO or behind held work, some rung and only read (in flight); IOSurface mapper surfaces filled by the GPU through mapper buffers and presented with opcode 8, and task 0's backings presented with opcode 7 (scanout); cursors; a faulted worker child (randfault); and in some programs the EFI framebuffer at boot. The event is pause and unpause (with work rung while paused and a new mode list), the suspend sequence onto a fresh device (with work rung after rvg_device_will_suspend), rvg_device_will_suspend then unpause (no display), reset, stop, a fresh device, the root FIFO disabled and enabled again, a version change or a dip below EFI's version, the host unplugging and plugging a display or changing its modes, a worker child deleted with its work and defined again, or nothing. The guest then resolves it as a driver would: opens the gate, recovers the fault the way the event left it, redoes its set-up and rings abandoned work again.  The model predicts every observation: every item's slot bytes and every surface's bytes (written into mirror pages and compared), each stamp (waited for; held ones time out in waits labelled [held]), the fault registers after each recovery, one new_frame per present and the resume's replays of glyphs, shows and mappings. Programs with mapper surfaces run in the map and isolate setups, the others in all three; some programs end in a death docs/testing.md 7.3 lists, with work held, as their last action.  This program: ports=0, surfaces=0, workers=4, fault rate 0.2; 4 rounds.
